════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 Reading guide
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

 Each row in the per-substrate tables is one agent class:
 a (kind, target-kind) pair that grouped the episodes.
 The columns describe that class's complete observable
 behaviour over the run:

   eps     — number of agent instances of this class
             that fired (= number of dispatches)
   succ    — agents that produced positive abstraction-
             score delta (or returned explicit success)
   succ%   — class-level success rate ("agent confidence
             of this class", per ADR 0076)
   first/last tick — temporal envelope of the class's
             activity ("agent attention window")
   mean Δ  — average score impact per dispatch

 The recent-window attention share approximates which
 agent class is currently "holding the floor" in the
 global workspace.

 Constitution heavy reading is preserved: nothing in
 this output is new ontology — these are queries over
 Memory::episodes that already exist. Agents are
 behaviour patterns we read off the log, not entities
 the system declares.

